> As your XP level rises, more difficult creatures are generated.  Most
> people consider the benefits for a higher XP to be outweighed by this
> drawback, especially for non-spellcasters.

I believe the precise method is, average together the player's
experience level and the current dungeon level, and that's the target
challenge factor for generating monsters.  You can still get monsters
weaker than that factor (with an extra hit die), and also monsters a
little stronger (minus one HD).  There are other factors (groups
sizes, for instance) and some subtleties (if you have the Amulet,
"current dungeon level" is replaced with the deepest level reached),
but that works most of the time.

When you first enter a level, I believe all the monsters on it are
generated according to your level at that moment.  So, it may be
slightly to your advantage to quickly dip down and back up the down
stairs when you find it, even if you plan on exploring the current
level more, or going back for the mines or Sokoban, especially at
shallow depths with physically-weak classes.

One problem with attempting a low-level game is that it won't freeze
monster difficulty, just slow it down.  If you're level 5 on Dungeon
Level 41, monsters will still be generated at challenge level 23. 
There are also monsters in the game of set difficulty levels, that get
generated regardless of this factor.  For example, mazes always have
minotaurs, demon lords and princes have set (very high) challenge
factors, certain levels with pre-built structures have set-in-stone
monsters on them, and the Mines always contain dwarfs and gnomes.  And
special rooms often have a large quantity of monsters in them that
ignore the monster generation difficulty factor.

Furthermore, a good number of monsters that appear are generated on
the fly, as anyone encountering a water demon from drinking from a
fountain on level 1 will attest.  Of course many of these sources
won't matter once the player has learned the First Rule of Nethack
Survival.  (That is: Don't drink from the fountains!)

The way I see it, once you learn the game and the basic tricks to
survival, there are two primary killers of early characters:
#1: Encountering one of a small number of monsters that can quickly
end a player's life.  Heading the list is the illustrious Soldier Ant,
raider of picnics, veteran of wars, who is so fast that it usually
gets multiple hits per player move and can almost always outrun the
player at this stage of the game.  These monsters have a slightly low
challenge rating.  (Not that I'm suggesting changing it -- in my
opinion, running into beasties like this is part of what makes
Nethack, Nethack.)
#2: Failing to gain good equipment by the time monsters get too strong
for a player with starting equipment.  Equipment, generally, is a more
relevant measure of character might than experience level.  Handling a
soldier ant is no problem with speed boots.  Killer bees lose much of
their sting (har har) if you've gotten poison resistance.  A mumak is
just a giant hamburger if you got a wand of death.  This puts the
player at the direct mercy of the random number generator.

Overcoming these issues is the primary challenge of the early game.

